WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
ASK NOT TO TRACK:
Two years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.060
Apple shocked the world with the introduction of&nbsp;
“Ask Not To Track”. For the first time in forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.340
a big tech company was siding with the people&nbsp;
and preventing data collection. This update&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.620
had real consequences as well. Facebook, for&nbsp;
example, was losing as much as $10 billion per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:23.040
year in revenue just because of that little pop&nbsp;
up. This isn’t too surprising though as 96% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.860
iOS users were disabling in app tracking despite&nbsp;
Mark’s pleas that they opt in. In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:34.260
Apple was receiving a bunch of praise for giving&nbsp;
users the choice. Of course, no one was stupid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.660
enough to think that Apple was actually doing&nbsp;
this for the people, but it was concluded that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.700
this was mainly just a marketing strategy. Apple&nbsp;
has always advertised the iPhone with the allure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:50.400
of privacy. So, this move would surely strengthen&nbsp;
Apple’s brand loyalty and might even convert some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.940
android users. But, what if I told you that all&nbsp;
of that was just stage one? You see, pushing out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:03.420
Facebook and Google wasn’t actually a defensive&nbsp;
move, it was an offensive move. Now that Apple has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.580
effectively shut off everyone else from collecting&nbsp;
deep data on iOS users, guess who advertisers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:14.340
to rely on to get access to iOS users. The&nbsp;
answer is Apple. This wasn’t just something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:19.560
that Apple stumbled into either. While they like&nbsp;
to portray themselves as the hardware giant that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.900
just wants to create the best products possible,&nbsp;
Apple is actually becoming a software company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.300
and the reasoning is obvious. It’s way more&nbsp;
profitable. You know how Apple charges ludicrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.700
amounts for their products? Well, even with that&nbsp;
pricing, Apple’s gross margin on their products is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:42.420
only 37%. Their gross margin on their seemingly&nbsp;
cheap services, however, is a whopping 71%. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.940
looks like offering services like Apple Music and&nbsp;
iCloud not only garners less criticism but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.640
way more profitable as well. But, by for the most&nbsp;
profitable service in the world is serving ads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:00.000
and Apple wants a piece of this pie. So, here’s&nbsp;
Apple’s master plan to takeover iOS advertising.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:09.000
FALLING SALES:
This might seem like a recent development, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.660
this story actually dates back nearly ten years to&nbsp;
September 19, 2014. This was the long anticipated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:21.480
release date of the iPhone 6. Rumors had been&nbsp;
flying wild for months, people had been camping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:28.140
outside Apple stores for days, and the hype was&nbsp;
unreal. Apple was finally releasing larger format&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.540
iPhones. In the meantime, the general public was&nbsp;
finally coming around to the idea of spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.460
several hundred dollars on a smartphone, and for&nbsp;
many, the iPhone 6 was their first smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:44.280
including myself. All of this culminated in&nbsp;
Apple selling a ridiculous 220 million iPhones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:52.020
6s and 6 pluses. Apple had done better than ever&nbsp;
before, but this was the peak of iPhone sales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:58.980
Every year after that, iPhone sales inched down&nbsp;
step by step. This wasn’t really Apple’s fault.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:04.080
The reality was that smartphone progression was&nbsp;
rapidly slowing down, phones lasted for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:10.680
and everyone who wanted an iPhone already had&nbsp;
one. But, while this was a reasonable explanation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:16.860
Apple couldn’t just tell this to shareholders.&nbsp;
If Tim Apple had done this, he probably would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.020
gotten the boot, and be replaced by someone&nbsp;
who was quote on quote more ambitious. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:27.300
Tim had to do something. His first instinct was&nbsp;
to raise prices. I mean, if you wanna make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:33.480
same revenue off of less units, the easy way out&nbsp;
is to just raise prices. With the iPhone 7, Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.580
increased the price of the plus series by $20,&nbsp;
and with the iPhone 8, Apple increased the price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:45.240
of both series by a total $50. But, by far the&nbsp;
biggest jump in price came with the iPhone X which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:50.700
came in at $1000. In the western world, this phone&nbsp;
was memed as the thousand dollar emoji machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.860
but this phone actually sold extraordinarily&nbsp;
well in China ending Apple’s sales decline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:01.200
It seemed like the increased pricing was working,&nbsp;
and Apple would double down on this strategy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:08.580
their next generation. The iPhone XR would come in&nbsp;
at an eyewatering $750, and Apple introduced a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:15.180
max version that started at $1,100. We have since&nbsp;
gotten more used to these prices, but to this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.940
when adjusting for inflation, these were the most&nbsp;
expensive iPhones Apple has ever released. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:26.880
this quickly became evident in their performance.&nbsp;
Sales were terrible. In fact, they were so bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.340
that Apple didn’t even want to tell us how bad&nbsp;
they were. Instead, they would just go ahead and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:37.680
stop reporting unit sales numbers altogether.&nbsp;
Unsurprisingly, Apple would go ahead and reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.840
the starting price of the iPhone for the first&nbsp;
time ever with the iPhone 11. This led to a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:48.780
better response with the public, but this was&nbsp;
by no means an endgame for Apple. They were back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:54.600
to square one and they had to not only address&nbsp;
declining iPhone sales, but also find new ways to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.600 --> 00:05:00.120
grow. They were finding some success with Airpods&nbsp;
and the newer Macs, but what they really needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:06.720
was a full on new sector. As they looked around&nbsp;
them, the answer was obvious. Out of all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.540
tech giants, they were the only ones focusing on&nbsp;
hardware. Everyone else was focusing on software.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:20.040
ATTRACTING THE MASSES:
Apple likes to market themselves as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:25.440
premium company making premium priced products,&nbsp;
and based on public sentiment, it seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.480
this messaging has stuck really well. Everyone&nbsp;
views Apple products as expensive, luxurious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.960
and exclusive. But, ever since the disaster with&nbsp;
the iPhone Xr and Xs, Apple has actually been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:42.300
making their products more accessible than ever.&nbsp;
Back in the day, Apple would discontinue their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.040
previous iPhones as soon as the new version came&nbsp;
out. But, nowadays, they’re keeping around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:53.340
older generations for years, and they have models&nbsp;
priced as low as $429. In fact, if you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:58.980
the pricing of the SE, 12, and 13, it’s not much&nbsp;
different from the pricing of the Pixel series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:05.460
which is known for offering great value. Apple&nbsp;
will never advertise this though. As Google has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:11.760
found out first hand, most people don’t actually&nbsp;
want a value product. What they want is a premium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:17.640
exclusive product, but for a value price, and&nbsp;
this is precisely what Apple has been offering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:23.160
They have their fancy pro series, and their $1000&nbsp;
stand, and their $700 wheels to establish the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.920
status of their brand. But, their real revenue&nbsp;
drivers are their lower priced products. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.600
if you’re smart about your trade ins, you can get&nbsp;
an iPhone that’s 1 or 2 generations old from your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:40.080
carrier for $100 $200 bucks max. You might even&nbsp;
be able to get it for free. This strategy doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.000
just apply to iPhones either. Apple has made&nbsp;
their lower tier products value centric across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.720
the board. I mean, you can literally get the iPad&nbsp;
9th generation for $329. Even if you spring for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:56.700
the iPad Pro, it’s still substantially cheaper&nbsp;
than the flagship iPhones. The same thing can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:01.860
said about the Mac lineup as well. Apple was&nbsp;
already offering great value with the M1 Mac&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:08.580
Mini that came it at $700, but they’ve taken this&nbsp;
to another level M2. Despite all the inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:15.600
Apple didn’t just keep the price the same, but&nbsp;
they actually knocked it down to $600. At that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:22.680
price, the M2 Mac Mini is arguably the best value&nbsp;
desktop computer on the market period. You can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:28.740
similar trends with the Apple watch and Macbook&nbsp;
lineup as well, but, wait a minute, why in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:33.480
world is Apple doing this? Wouldn’t these lower&nbsp;
priced products just exacerbate their revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:40.140
and profit margins? Well, yes and no. Obviously,&nbsp;
these lower priced products will have much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:45.960
modest revenue per unit and margins, but they’ll&nbsp;
also sell better. This will keep the overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:52.680
revenue from each sector relatively stable or even&nbsp;
growing. But, net margin will indeed go down and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:58.920
that’s exactly what Apple experienced for much of&nbsp;
the 2010s. Between 2012 and 2020, Apple saw their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:05.880
net margin fall from 27% down to 20%. This might&nbsp;
not sound like a big deal, but when we’re talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:10.320
about hundreds of billions of dollars worth of&nbsp;
revenue, this translates to tens of billions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:15.480
dollars worth of less profit. This is why Apple’s&nbsp;
net income didn’t increase all that much during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:20.520
this time period despite their revenue nearly&nbsp;
doubling. Apple was ok with this though because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:25.980
they had something much bigger in the works,&nbsp;
and this was just stage one: user collection.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.900
SOFTWARE GIANT:&nbsp;

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:35.400
As Apple offered more value driven products and&nbsp;
locked more people into the Apple ecosystem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:41.400
they were simultaneously doubling down on their&nbsp;
services. Likely the most notable example is Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:47.940
music. Fun fact, Steve Jobs actually hated the&nbsp;
idea of music subscriptions. He said that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:53.940
Jesus couldn’t sell music subscriptions, and this&nbsp;
was really the premise of iTunes as whole. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:59.460
idea of iTunes was that people could buy virtually&nbsp;
any track in the world and keep it forever for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:05.760
reasonable 99 cents. But, Apple scrapped this idea&nbsp;
altogether in mid 2015 when they announced Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.740
music. If you’ve been living under a rock, Apple&nbsp;
music is a music streaming subscription similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:16.800
to Spotify. But, unlike Apple’s brand image, Apple&nbsp;
music was once again reasonably priced. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.200
Apple Music was priced identically to Spotify.&nbsp;
Pair this with Apple’s beautiful integration with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:28.140
their devices and you end up with 88 million users&nbsp;
paying you $10 or more every single month. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:33.840
that itself is nearly a billion dollars per month.&nbsp;
But, that’s nothing in comparison to their cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:39.900
business. iCloud is once again priced basically&nbsp;
identically to Google drive, but it comes along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:46.920
with the whole Apple aesthetic and convenience.&nbsp;
And boom, by 2018, Apple had an estimated 850&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:53.100
million iCloud users out of which 170 million were&nbsp;
paying. Apple had found a golden strategy. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:57.660
didn’t have to reinvent the wheel by any means.&nbsp;
They just had to take already popular services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:02.520
integrate it into the Apple world, and&nbsp;
offer it for the same price. And boom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:07.500
they suddenly had tens of millions if not hundreds&nbsp;
of millions of paying subscribers. They deployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:13.320
this same strategy with Apple TV, Apple Arcade,&nbsp;
Apple News, Apple Fitness, Apple Care, and who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:19.080
knows what else. This was working relatively well&nbsp;
by the end of the 2010s, but the real explosion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:24.840
didn’t come till the pandemic. Suddenly, everyone&nbsp;
was at home and they needed even more virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:31.140
entertainment than before. They needed more&nbsp;
music, more games, more TV, more media. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:36.300
also had a bunch of money saved up from not going&nbsp;
on vacation and not going out nearly as often. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:41.100
they resorted to spending much of their money on&nbsp;
tech and guess who was in the perfect position to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:47.220
take advantage of this? Well, it was actually all&nbsp;
of the big tech companies which of course included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:52.380
Apple. Within the 12 months following the start&nbsp;
of the pandemic, Apple’s revenue grew by over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:59.880
$100 billion and their net income nearly doubled.&nbsp;
And today, Apple boasts an impressive 935 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:05.760
paid subscribers world wide. In other words, ⅛&nbsp;
of the world has some sort of paid Apple based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:11.400
subscription. Apple had done it, they successfully&nbsp;
transitioned from being a hardware giant to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.460
a hardware/software giant. But, Apple wasn’t&nbsp;
satisfied. They wanted to take it one step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:22.560
further as they were still missing out on the&nbsp;
most lucrative service of them all: advertising.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:24.900
BRING IN THE ADS:&nbsp;

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:32.220
Apple wasn’t just gonna start offering ads&nbsp;
like Google or Facebook though. As we all know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:38.160
Apple likes to do everything the Apple way with&nbsp;
finesse and clever positioning. So, they decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.980
to leverage public angst. The shady habits of&nbsp;
Google and Facebok were no longer a secret. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.020
business practices were very much public knowledge&nbsp;
and people hated them for it. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:55.320
people didn’t hate it enough to switch, but they&nbsp;
did hate it enough to complain. So, what better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:00.180
way to create an advertising monopoly than to&nbsp;
side with the public and block out external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:06.480
data collection. This was a win win win win&nbsp;
scenario. One, Apple was hurting the brand image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.980
of their competitors by basically suggesting&nbsp;
that what they were doing was wrong. Two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:16.020
they were hurting the revenue and bottom line&nbsp;
of their competitors big time. Three, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:21.480
bolstering their own brand image as the privacy&nbsp;
king. And four, they had ultimate control over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:27.120
all advertising within the Apple ecosystem. We&nbsp;
should also note that Apple has been experimenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.860
with more tame advertising for several years&nbsp;
now. They’ve been selling ad spots within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:38.100
App Store and Apple News since 2016 actually. So,&nbsp;
they’re be no means complete newbies to the space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:44.340
but they’re about to this to the next level. Since&nbsp;
August, Apple has been hiring a substantial number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:49.560
of engineers to work on some sort of large&nbsp;
scale ad platform. It’s expected that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:55.260
new platform will essentially be Apple’s version&nbsp;
of Google or Facebook Ads. But, wait a minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.260 --> 00:13:00.720
Apple can’t just bash on Google and Facebook for&nbsp;
years and turn around and do the exact same thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:07.020
right? I mean, Tim Cook himself has argued that ad&nbsp;
driven business models are inherently invasive of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:13.140
privacy. So, how could Apple make such a pivot&nbsp;
without destroying their branding? Well, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:18.420
answer is likely by introducing an Apple twist.&nbsp;
In other words, they’ll throw in some encryption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:23.340
some decentralization, and some anonymity and&nbsp;
they’ll frame it as something that protects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:29.460
privacy. We can already see something along these&nbsp;
with their hide my email feature. If you’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:33.780
familiar with this feature, it basically allows&nbsp;
you to generate a proxy email address when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:38.940
signing up for websites or emailing people. Apple&nbsp;
doesn’t read or process any of the emails that go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:43.800
through this proxy address. I suspect that they’ll&nbsp;
implement something similar when it comes to ads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:48.960
Maybe all of the data that they collect will be&nbsp;
stored locally on your device which will check off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:54.060
the decentralization checkbox. After that, maybe&nbsp;
they’ll use some sort of anonymous proxy tunnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:59.640
that’s end to end encrypted to connect advertisers&nbsp;
to your device. This way, they can claim that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:05.100
nor the advertisers know your data themselves.&nbsp;
They’re simply a proxy that connects advertisers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:11.000
and end consumers while protecting your privacy&nbsp;
or atleast that’s what the pitch will probably be.

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:16.920
THE FUTURE OF APPLE:
In the end, Apple was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:21.000
only mega tech company that didn’t drive most&nbsp;
of their revenue from software and services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:26.520
but over the past several years, this has&nbsp;
been rapidly changing. The reality is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:31.440
smartphones have hit a plateau and Apple can&nbsp;
only introduce the next iPhone that’s better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:37.620
than ever before and increase the price so many&nbsp;
times. In fact, I believe that it’s just a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:42.240
of time until Apple drops the number naming&nbsp;
scheme on the iPhone. Before you know it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:48.360
the new iPhone will be called the 2027 iPhone&nbsp;
as opposed to the iPhone 19. This will no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:54.960
reduce the hype surrounding iPhone launches,&nbsp;
but that’s ok. By then Apple will not only be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.960 --> 00:15:00.480
a software giant, but they will be a full on&nbsp;
software company. Their iPhones, Macs, iPads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.400
and Watches will simply be a vehicle to sell their&nbsp;
real money producers: subscriptions and services.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:10.860
It looks like the latest effort in making this&nbsp;
vision a reality is getting into ads, and that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:15.360
why Apple really shut out external tracking.&nbsp;
What do you think about Apple getting into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:21.240
ad business? Comment that down below. Also, drop&nbsp;
a like if you respect the business decision by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:25.800
hate the implications. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

